# Actuator endpoints are not secured by default

If you have Spring Security in your app, you should explicitly secure them:

```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.authorizeRequests()
        .antMatchers("/actuator/health").permitAll()
        .antMatchers("/actuator/info").permitAll()
        .antMatchers("/actuator/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
        .anyRequest().authenticated()
        .and()
        .httpBasic();
    return http.build();
}
```

You can add custom information to the `/info` endpoint via `application.properties` or a `InfoContributor` bean.

**Via properties:**
```properties
info.app.name=My Cool App
info.app.version=1.0.0
info.app.description=Spring Boot Demo
```

**Via Java bean:**
```java
@Component
public class CustomInfoContributor implements InfoContributor {
    @Override
    public void contribute(Info.Builder builder) {
        builder.withDetail("build", Map.of("artifact", "demo", "version", "1.0.0"));
    }
}
```

Add your own health check by implementing `HealthIndicator`:

```java
@Component
public class CustomHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        // Perform custom check, e.g., external service reachable
        boolean up = checkExternalService();
        if (up) {
            return Health.up().withDetail("external", "available").build();
        } else {
            return Health.down().withDetail("external", "unavailable").build();
        }
    }

    private boolean checkExternalService() {
        // your logic
        return true;
    }
}
```

Now `/health` will include a `custom` component with the status.
